Subject: librtlsdr: fail rtlsdr_open instead of resetting the device

rtlsdr_open() performs a dummy register write and, when it fails, calls
libusb_reset_device() and carries on. On the RTL-SDR Blog V4 that USB
reset is fatal when the dongle is already stalled: on parallax at
23:59:45 on 17 Sep 2026 the reset was followed by "device descriptor
read/64, error -110" and "device not accepting address, error -62",
and the dongle never enumerated again, not after four hub-port power
cycles, a 30 s port cut or an xHCI controller rebind. The open now
fails instead; MeshSat's reader backs off and the device-health ladder
escalates to a hub-port power cycle. [MESHSAT-1222]

--- a/src/librtlsdr.c
+++ b/src/librtlsdr.c
@@ -1581,10 +1581,15 @@
 
 	dev->rtl_xtal = DEF_RTL_XTAL_FREQ;
 
-	/* perform a dummy write, if it fails, reset the device */
+	/* perform a dummy write; if it fails, fail the open. MeshSat does
+	 * not reset the device here: a USB reset of a stalled RTL-SDR Blog
+	 * V4 left it unable to enumerate until its power was removed
+	 * (parallax, 17 Sep 2026). The caller backs off and escalates to a
+	 * hub-port power cycle instead. [MESHSAT-1222] */
 	if (rtlsdr_write_reg(dev, USBB, USB_SYSCTL, 0x09, 1) < 0) {
-		fprintf(stderr, "Resetting device...\n");
-		libusb_reset_device(dev->devh);
+		fprintf(stderr, "Dummy write failed, not resetting the device\n");
+		r = -EIO;
+		goto err;
 	}
 
 	rtlsdr_init_baseband(dev);
